<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Finance</RootNamespace>
		<Description>FIX 4.4 tag-value parsing built with DotGram.</Description>

		<PackageReleaseNotes>0.2.0 replaces the public surface. This is what to write instead, line by line; everything below was read from the published 0.1.0 assembly rather than remembered. THERE IS ONE DOOR, AND IT IS NOT THE OLD ONE. 0.1.0 had Fix44 with eight methods; 0.2.0 has FixParser, and the name of the call says what comes back: the verb says where the input is and what happens to it (Parse takes a buffer whole, Read consumes a reader or a stream and leaves it open), and the plural says how many. Fix44.Parse(wire) and Fix44.Parse(wire, FixParseMode.Strict) -> FixParser.ParseMessage(wire) followed by message.Validate(), which answers with everything wrong rather than throwing on the first. Fix44.Parse(wire, FixParseMode.Lenient) -> FixParser.ParseMessage(wire), which is now what parsing means. Fix44.TryParse(wire, out m, out e, mode) -> FixParser.TryParseMessage(wire, out m, out e). From a reader or a stream the same two are ReadMessage and TryReadMessage; ReadMessages walks a stream of them, ParseMessages reads every message of a buffer, and BuildMessage puts one together from fields already read. And there is an answer 0.1.0 did not have: ParseFields and ReadFields return flat typed fields from a string, a byte array, ReadOnlyMemory&lt;byte&gt;, a TextReader or a Stream, without assembling a message. THE TYPES YOU NAME. NewOrderSingle, ExecutionReport and the other 91 message classes -> FixMessage.NewOrderSingle and so on: they are nested in FixMessage now, a closed set whose base stands in front of every arm of a switch, which is how this package writes its field hierarchy too. CustomFixMessage -> FixMessage.Custom, since a nested case does not repeat the name it is nested in. A message's own properties are untouched: text is still string, numbers are still FixNumber?, and Header, Trailer, AllFields, OriginalWire, GetField and GetGroup keep their names and their meaning. What changes under them is two type names. The 81 typed group-entry classes (PartiesEntry, AllocsEntry and the rest) are withdrawn, so order.Parties is an IReadOnlyList&lt;FixFieldSet&gt; read with GetField and GetGroup rather than a list of a class per group. And the field view is renamed: 0.1.0's FixField -- the readonly struct with Tag, Value, Wire, Position, ValuePosition, Length, TryGetDecimal and TryGetInt64 -- is now FixFieldView, member for member, with TypedValue added. The name FixField is reused for something else: the closed set of typed field cases the new field layer returns (FixField.Custom, FixField.RawData, FixField.Symbol and the rest), which is what FixFieldView.TypedValue hands you. THE CONTEXT. new FixParseOptions(mode, pairs) -> new FixContext { LengthDataPairs = pairs }, one value that reads the input and holds the message to its schema, and the pairs are an IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;int,int&gt; from length tag to data tag rather than a FixDataPair[]. A supplied dictionary now ADDS to the standard's sixteen pairs instead of replacing them, so one that meant to add can drop everything it was repeating. A pair is read as the two fields it is on the wire, the length and then the data, each with its own position; 0.1.0 returned the data alone. FixParseMode is gone, and with it FixParseOptions.Mode: reading the wire and holding it to a schema are two acts. Parse builds whatever it can read; message.Validate() reports everything wrong at once, each finding naming its rule, its scope, its tag, the entry of the repeating group it is in, and where in the source it begins. So a message Strict used to turn away is now returned and reported on: a required field absent, a required component empty, a value outside its type or code set, a tag twice in one scope, a tag the schema does not place in that scope, a group's fields out of order, an unknown MsgType, and MessageEncoding absent beside an Encoded field. What still refuses is what the reader cannot defer: framing, BodyLength, CheckSum, a field it could not read, a length not followed by its data, a NumInGroup that sizes an array past the fields left, and a group entry that does not begin with its delimiter. LOG FRAMING IS A VALUE. There is no ParseLog: ParseLog(text) -> ParseFields(text, FixContext.Log), and context with { Framing = FixFraming.Log } reads your own pairs in log framing. Which separator an input uses is a property of the input. OPTIONS ARE AN OPTIONAL ARGUMENT, not a second method, so the door has fourteen entries where the pair-per-setting shape would have twenty-five. Two call shapes change silently rather than failing to compile: ReadFields(reader, 4096) would bind 4096 to nothing and must be ReadFields(reader, null, 4096), and TryParseMessage(input, out m, out e, null) once threw and now reads with the defaults. WHAT IS NEW BESIDES. A streamed call is read through the context's BufferSize and bounded by its MaxRetained, and a field past the limit is an IOException in both the generated and the hand parser. Numbers are decimal. Every public member is documented and a package skill ships beside the README. FixContext takes a FixFieldFactory that answers a consumer's own tag with its type, tag =&gt; tag == 25005 ? FixCustom.Integer : null, and the field is then a FixField.Custom&lt;long&gt;, or a class of the consumer's derived from it; a loaded dictionary answers for its own fields first. A FixMessageFactory, MsgType =&gt; FixCustomMessage?, builds what the package does not define; where it answers null, or a tag is declared by nothing, the field is a FixField.Invalid of its tag and the message a FixMessage.Invalid. VALIDATION HANGS ON THE CLASS. A message type and a class are the same thing here, so the rule lives in the class's own field: message.Validate() asks FixMessage.NewOrderSingle.Rule, replacing a rule is assigning to that field, and undoing it is assigning the name back -- FixValidator.ValidateNewOrderSingle. FixValidator is not a way in; it holds the ninety-four rules this package compiles in, one named method a type, so that a replacement can be taken back. FixParser.LoadDictionary(stream) reads a counterparty's QuickFIX dictionary and writes the rule of every type it describes; it answers with nothing and throws where the file is not a dictionary it accepts, because half of one schema and half of another is worse than a refusal at the door. TWO CONSEQUENCES TO KNOW. One field a class means one configuration for the process: two counterparties with two schemas at once is not expressible. And a dictionary does not make a type known that this package has no class for -- such a type has no field for a rule to live in, so it is still reported as unknown, and what to do about it is to write the class. </PackageReleaseNotes>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>fix;finance;trading;parser;dotgram</PackageTags>

		<!-- The package ships its XML documentation, and every public member has some:
		     under TreatWarningsAsErrors a member left undocumented fails the build. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Handwritten" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44.Tests" />

		<!-- The benchmarks read the composed rule text and compile it, which needs the tables and
		     the nodes the text is written against. Nothing ships from that project. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Benchmarks" />

		<ProjectReference Include="../DotGram/DotGram.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

		<!-- A dictionary loaded at run time is turned into the same straight-line checks the package
		     compiles in, written as text and compiled by the expression language: one lambda a
		     slot, typed on the class it checks. -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../DotGram.ExpressionLanguage/DotGram.ExpressionLanguage.csproj" />

		<AdditionalFiles Include="Fix44/*.gram" />

		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<!-- The skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has restored
		     this package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README
		     says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<None Include="../../LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="licenses/DotGram/" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />
		<PackageReference Include="Portable.System.DateTimeOnly" />
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
